jtest: the ext2fs journal on a Linux host
=========================================

Builds ext2fs/journal.c straight from the Hurd tree (HURD=, default
../cross-hurd/src/hurd), unchanged, and runs it against a fake ext2fs:

  shim/        A fake Mach and the <hurd/...> types the real Hurd lib
               headers need.  libdiskfs, libports, libstore, libihash and
               ext2fs headers are the real ones, so struct node, struct
               store, diskfs_transaction_t are what ext2fs sees.
  env/         The fake ext2fs (fakefs.c) and the harness API (jtest.h):
               - the store is the image in memory, and every write and
                 sync also goes to a write log;
               - the disk cache maps every block; blocks are resident or
                 evicted, and tests edit them with jt_edit, which faults
                 an evicted block back in;
               - a second cache stands for the directories' file
                 pagers, the only live copy of a directory block; it
                 writes with DISK_CACHE 0, and tests edit it the way
                 dir.c does (jt_dir_edit, jt_dir_mark_dirty);
               - pager threads serve clean/evict page-outs through
                 journal_store_write and page-ins through
                 journal_store_read; a page-in must return exactly the
                 bytes that were evicted, or it counts as a stale read.
  env/crash.c  jt_crash_check: rebuilds the image after prefixes of the
               write log (and with the last write torn), replays each with
               the real e2fsck -fy, requires e2fsck -fn clean, then runs
               the test's own check.  Images are checked in parallel in
               /dev/shm.  A failure keeps the crashed image, e2fsck's
               replay of it, and a decoded write log (JBD2 headers,
               descriptor tags, home writes) under build/.
  tests/       t_basic: many transactions under page-outs and evictions,
               clean unmount, every block home, e2fsck -fn clean.
               t_crash: one handle per transaction, each committed.
               t_wrap: goes round the smallest log ~8 times; forced
               checkpoints, and handles larger than a transaction may be.
               t_threads: 4 RPC threads share transactions, with nested
               starts and a shared block edited under a lock.
               t_dirs: directory blocks through the file pager, with
               journal_*_external, reservations given up, and syncs and
               evictions in the middle of an edit.
               t_notify_race: forces a pager home write to race a
               reservation in a draining (T_LOCKED) transaction, holding
               the write in store_write (jt_store_write_hook) and waiting
               for the state through env/journal_peek.c, which compiles
               journal.c with accessors to its internals.
               t_sweep_race: holds the stop-time sweep right before it
               copies a block, has a joining handle half-edit the block,
               and checks the torn copy is redone before commit.
               t_alloc: a model of balloc.c over a pool of blocks: frees
               with journal_record_freed_blocks, busy until
               ext2_release_busy_blocks, journal_wait_freed_blocks when
               none is free, and blocks reused as metadata and as file
               data.  An old journal copy of a block must never be
               replayed or checkpointed over its new owner.
               t_forget_race: holds kjournald's checkpoint write of a
               freed block's old copy while the block's next owner
               commits; journal_forget_freed_blocks must have retired
               that copy when the free committed.
               t_free_only: transactions whose only change is a free must
               commit, on a commit and at the unmount: revoke records
               logged, blocks handed back.
               t_pager_txn: pager threads that start transactions, as
               pager_unlock_page does for a fault from an RPC thread that
               holds a handle: the pager joins a draining transaction,
               journal_wait_freed_blocks never waits on it, and its
               blocks land in the faulting operation's transaction.  A
               watchdog turns a deadlock into a failure.
               t_balloc: the real balloc.c (BALLOC_C=, default the
               tree's), linked only into t_balloc*: two files grow and
               shrink through ext2_new_block and ext2_free_blocks, their
               inodes and indirect blocks journaled, data written home
               between operations, and a filler thread turning the log
               over.  Crash images must be e2fsck clean, the superblock's
               free count that of the groups, and each file in the state
               of one of its operations.  Runs on build/balloc.img.
               t_sync: diskfs_sync_everything at any moment
               (jt_sync_everything mirrors pager.c with a journal): while
               a commit drains, while the pager serves faults, waiting or
               not, and with nothing to write while another commit is
               still writing.  Every operation stopped before a sync that
               returned, the inodes it wrote, and with a waiting sync the
               file data written before it, must survive any crash.
               t_remount: a second mount on a log full of the first
               mount's transactions, after a clean unmount, or after a
               crash and e2fsck's replay; and with both mounts' log
               layouts lined up, so that a reused sequence number would
               replay the first mount's transactions.
               Each crash image must hold whole operations only, and
               every operation stopped before a commit that returned.
  patches/     Fixes found with the harness, against ext2fs/journal.c.

  make check                      build everything and run the tests
  make check SAN=address,undefined
  make check SAN=thread
  make check JOURNAL_C=patched/journal.c   test a modified copy
  JT_CRASH_POINTS=0 build/t_wrap  # every crash point (default: 300)
  JT_NO_CRASH=1 build/t_threads 7 # run seed 7, skip the crash images
  JT_JOBS=1, JT_TMP=dir           # crash-check workers, image directory
  JT_WRITE_DELAY=200              # each store write takes up to 200 us,
                                  # so that writes in flight overlap

To try a patch:
  mkdir -p patched && cp $HURD/ext2fs/journal.c patched/ &&
  patch -d patched -p2 < patches/empty-log-superblock.diff

A test's stderr (journal warnings, statistics, the flight recorder dump)
goes to build/t_NAME.log.  Needs gcc and e2fsprogs on the host.

The pager follows libpager: writes of a block are serialized, and a
page-in that comes while the block is being written out gets the data
being written (PM_PAGEINWAIT) instead of reading it.

On a stale page-in, the harness prints the block's last events (edits,
page-outs, page-ins, reservations, marks; jt_trace adds more).

Known TSan report: the stop-time sweep copies a disk-cache block while
a thread that joined during the unlocked sweep edits it.  The torn copy
never commits: the editor's journal_get_write_access and
journal_mark_dirty each set needs_copy again, and the last handle to
stop sweeps again.  t_sweep_race forces it and checks that.

jt_pager_call runs a callback on a pager thread, for the pager
callbacks that start transactions.  jt_file_* write file data through
the file pager; jt_dir_* mark the block as a directory's, which
jt_sync_everything does not write out when there is a journal.

For balloc.c, the harness has the rest of ext2fs it needs: a separate
in-core superblock with hyper.c's journaled diskfs_set_hypermetadata
(copied by hand: keep it in step with hyper.c),
disk cache references, pokel_add marking pages dirty.  balloc.c writes
the disk cache without faulting, so a referenced block (and the
superblock and group descriptors) is never evicted in the harness.

What is not modelled: pages larger
than a block, and anything above the journal API.  Test images are
mke2fs -t ext3 (indirect-mapped journal inode, no extents).
